      {
        "id": 44323,
        "name": "Violent Soldier",
        "summary": "Sinistron, released as Violent Soldier in Japan, is a side-scrolling, ship based shooter that was released for the TurboGrafx-16 in 1991.\n\nThe game is notable, among other things, for the adjustable armored jaws of the player's spacecraft. Opening the invincible jaws of the ship increases the spread of the player's shots but exposes the vulnerable cockpit. Obtaining one weapon upgrade allows the ship's jaws to be set half-way open (a 3-shot spread) or closed (with increased damage), and a second upgrade allows them to be closed, half open, or fully open (a 5 shot spread).\n\nFive power-ups exist in the game. The Vulcan flame cannon, crystal-pulse laser, and CHAOS (homing) missiles are weapon upgrades. There are also speed upgrades and plasma droids, invincible pods which will flank the ship. The plasma droids can absorb enemy pellets and will even damage enemies they come into contact with.\n\nThe ship's charge-up attack releases a circular wave of force that will damage all enemies in a radius around the ship.\n\nSinistron has six stages of quickly increasing difficulty. The last two stages take place inside the Sinistron creature itself. The stages are:\n\nMechanical Stage\nGas Tunnel Stage\nBiological Stage\nDeep Space Stage\nOrgans Stage\nCerebral Cortex Stage",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7mri.jpg",
        "release_date": 661132800,
        "rating": null,
        "rating_count": null,
        "genres": "Shooter",
        "platform_ids": "86",
        "external_url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/violent-soldier",
        "cached_at": 1772527935
      },
      {
        "id": 44325,
        "name": "Dai Senpu",
        "summary": "Daisenpū is a vertical shooter by Nec Interchannel and conversion of the arcade game of the same name (also known as Twin Hawk) originally released by Toaplan in 1989. In the year 193X, country R is overwhelmed by hostile enemy forces and the small oasis town 'S' is the main target of the attack. A handful of survivors escape to the jungle and start working on a counter attack - but they are soon discovered and are now running out of time, and they must launch the Daisenpū squadron before it is fully operational. The player takes control of a fighter plane and must fly deep into enemy territory, attack ground targets such as tanks, turrets and warships (the game curiously doesn't feature any flying enemies and no obstacles to fly into, beside enemy bullets). The plane starts equipped with a limited double-shot machine gun that can be slowly upgraded throughout the game by collecting power-pods left behind by small trucks. But the originality of Daisenpū lies elsewhere - the player can call a small squadron of six planes and have them fly in formation, shooting anything in sight and crashing into an enemy unit when defeated. The squadron can also commit a Kamikaze attack at anytime and destroy ground units, or combine to release a devastative bomb-attack. Finally, Daisenpū offers an unusual feature - levels do not pause between them and reveal instead a continuous flow of undisturbed shooting action.",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6xdx.jpg",
        "release_date": 726969600,
        "rating": null,
        "rating_count": null,
        "genres": "Shooter",
        "platform_ids": "86",
        "external_url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/dai-senpu",
        "cached_at": 1772527935
      },
      {
        "id": 44326,
        "name": "F1 Pilot",
        "summary": "A Formula 1 racing game from Pack-In-Video, developed for the PC Engine. It was never released outside of Japan.\n\nF-1 Pilot is a F1 racing game for the PC Engine, one that depicts the racing from the driver's seat of the vehicle. The player is able to select a car before a race, but they all appear to be functionally identical except for the color. The player can also choose to have engine noises or BGM, but not both. Selecting either of the BGM modes will disable all sound effects from the game, including tire screeching and crashing noises.\n\nThe game has some odd uses of English (the game was never fully localized into English): For instance, it refers to F1 drivers as \"pilots\" and the title screen affirms that the player is the King of Kings.",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6xej.jpg",
        "release_date": 606614400,
        "rating": null,
        "rating_count": null,
        "genres": "Racing|Sport",
        "platform_ids": "86",
        "external_url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/f1-pilot",
        "cached_at": 1772527935
      },
      {
        "id": 44327,
        "name": "F1 Dream",
        "summary": "F-1 Dream is an automobile racing game for the PC Engine released exclusively in Japan in 1988.\n\nThe graphics are identical to the arcade game, even down to the pit crew and lane and fans in the stands. The controls are the same as well: The d-pad controls all eight ways, and the two buttons work as both gas-and-brake and as the transmission. Press the II button to launch the car, and once first gear tops out, quick switch to the I button. If your car is Turbo-equipped, once second gear tops out, hold down both buttons to kick in the afterburner. Letting go of the top gear button will brake the car, and hitting the button again will resume acceleration. The game includes back-markers as well. If you're leading the race or you and an opponent are well ahead of the pack, slower white cars will appear on the track to make things interesting for you, usually just as you're coming up to a particularly tough corner, or drifting across your lane on a straight and forcing you to make a course correction. They're a minor annoyance, but one to keep you on your toes when you've left everyone else in your dust.\n\nThe console game designers must have thought that the game needed to be expanded upon, because everything else about the game, outside of the actual racing, is totally different. Instead of jumping right into the F3000 circuit, they've turned it more into a career-mode. You start with street racing your Porsche 911 in races for cash, matching up head to head with another racer and betting on the result. Money that you win in these races can be used to hire yourself a pit crew that comes with you no matter where you're racing. Once you've proven yourself on the street (or you blow all your money, strangely), you get the call: F3000 wants you!",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6bpj.jpg",
        "release_date": 622684800,
        "rating": null,
        "rating_count": null,
        "genres": "Racing",
        "platform_ids": "86",
        "external_url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/f1-dream",
        "cached_at": 1772527935
      },
      {
        "id": 44328,
        "name": "F1 Circus '91",
        "summary": "A Formula One licensed racing game for the PC Engine and the second game in Nichibutsu's F1 Circus series.\n\nF1 Circus '91 is a top-down Formula One racing game with a strong simulation element. Players kit out their vehicle, take on various prominent Formula One competitors of the era in races that track the vehicle's damage and fuel. Due to the high speeds involved, players need to pay attention to flashing icons that indicate turns coming up in the road.\n\nThe game followed 1990's F1 Circus and was followed by 1992's F1 Circus '92. The series would then move to the Super Famicom as Super F1 Circus.",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6xeh.jpg",
        "release_date": 679276800,
        "rating": null,
        "rating_count": null,
        "genres": "Racing|Sport",
        "platform_ids": "86",
        "external_url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/f1-circus-91",
        "cached_at": 1772527935
      },
      {
        "id": 44329,
        "name": "Formation Soccer on J.League",
        "summary": "A soccer game for the PC Engine and part of Human's Formation Soccer series.\n\nFormation Soccer on J-League is a soccer game for the PC Engine and part of Human Entertainment's Formation Soccer series. The game focuses on Japan's top soccer league, the J-League, and features all ten teams active in the 1993-94 season. Formation Soccer on J-League was the penultimate game released officially on the PC Engine/TurboGrafx-16's HuCard format.\n\nThe game features a standard exhibition mode, a league mode that recreates the 1993-94 J-League, and an All-Stars mode that features two teams comprised of all the best players. The Exhibition and All-Stars modes can be played with up to four human players. The J-League mode allows for two players to be on the same team and take on the CPU-controlled league cooperatively.",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6xey.jpg",
        "release_date": 758592000,
        "rating": null,
        "rating_count": null,
        "genres": "Sport",
        "platform_ids": "86",
        "external_url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/formation-soccer-on-jleague",
        "cached_at": 1772527935
      },
      {
        "id": 44330,
        "name": "Ganbare Golf Boys",
        "summary": "Multiplayer golf for the PC Engine courtesy of Masaya and Dual.\n\nGanbare! Golf Boys (\"Do Your Best! Golf Boys\") is a PC Engine exclusive golf game that allows for up to four players to compete is a round of golf via the console's multi-tap peripheral. It did not receive a US localization.\n\nThe game has a permanent top-down view of each hole, zooming in whenever the player is on the green, and has the \"hit the sweet spot\" power gauge system standard to most golf simulators. It features a total of two courses, with eighteen holes apiece.\n\nGanbare! Golf Boys is notable for being the first project that prominent game musician Atsuhiro Motoyama ever worked on.",
        "cover_url": "https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6xf8.jpg",
